// @contract: C-105
// #2310's gate: the accumulator `acc + [x]` over a HEAP element grows in place,
// in both spellings. Before the fix the in-place append window refused a 4-byte
// handle slot and the concat path copied the whole list per step — 100,000
// String appends took 10.5 s against 11 ms for `Int`, and a 200,000-element
// accumulator reached C-197 (#2291 had to write `list.iterate` with `list.push`
// because of it).
//
// What this fixture pins is the CREDIT discipline, not the speed: every element
// is pushed EXACTLY once into the spine, so the join reads each string once and
// the length is exact. A missing +1 frees a live element (the reads below would
// read freed bytes); a doubled one leaks. The un-Dup'd-push spelling is C-186's
// fixture; this one is the growth chain.
//
// WHY 20,000 AND NOT A MILLION. Size is not this file's evidence, and a big
// number here is not free: spec/wasm_cross fixtures are also run by the
// INTERPRETER (the third oracle, tests/wasm_runtime_test_parts/interp_leg.rs),
// whose `acc + [x]` is a real copy — measured quadratic, ~1 s at 20,000 and
// ~40 min per million-element build. A million here does not make the gate
// stronger, it makes every corpus binary hang.
//
// WHAT CATCHES A REGRESSION AT THIS SIZE, measured A/B against develop without
// #2310 (same fixture, same machine): stdout is IDENTICAL on both — the outputs
// below cannot see this bug at all — while the allocation watermark reads
// 721,440 B before the fix against 459,328 B after, and wall time 0.78 s before
// against under 10 ms after. The ledger row is an EQUALITY pin, so that 1.6x
// drift fails loudly (crates/almide-wasm/tests/golden/alloc-baseline.txt); the
// wall-clock relation lives in scripts/check-perf-ratio.sh's strbuild rows.
//
// Note the shape of the memory number, because the obvious arithmetic is wrong:
// a per-step spine copy at n = 20,000 COPIES 4 · n²/2 ≈ 800 MB, but it does not
// ALLOCATE it — the outgrown spines are recycled through the free-list size
// classes (exact allocations only start above the 512 KB class, #1701), so the
// watermark moves by 1.6x rather than by orders of magnitude, and max RSS barely
// moves at all (9.2 MB against 9.0 MB). The cost of the quadratic form is time
// and churn, not footprint. An earlier draft of this comment claimed ~800 MB of
// ALLOCATION; measuring it is what corrected that.
fn build(n: Int, acc: List[String]) -> List[String] = {
  if n <= 0 then acc
  else build(n - 1, acc + ["ab"])
}

fn grow(n: Int) -> List[String] = {
  var out: List[String] = []
  var i = 0
  while i < n {
    out = out + ["ab"]
    i = i + 1
  }
  out
}

fn main() -> Unit = {
  let tail = build(20000, [])
  println("${list.len(tail)}")
  let loop_form = grow(20000)
  println("${list.len(loop_form)}")
  // The elements survive the growth chain: read one from each end of both.
  println("${tail[0]}${tail[19999]}${loop_form[0]}${loop_form[19999]}")
  // A borrowed element pushed from a var takes its own credit, and the source
  // stays readable afterwards — the shape the Dup discipline exists for.
  let s = "cd"
  var pair: List[String] = []
  pair = pair + [s]
  pair = pair + [s]
  println("${s}:${list.join(pair, ",")}")
}
